-- Passage rg6-authors-purpose-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('85785a5a-45db-5ed3-ba2b-1481042cc4ef', 'rg6-authors-purpose-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Put Recess Before Lunch$q$, $q$At Birchwood Elementary, students eat lunch first and go out to recess afterward. Many schools follow this order without thinking about it. I believe the order should be flipped, and I think our school board should try it next fall.

When recess comes after lunch, many students rush through their meals. They want to get outside quickly, so they leave half of their sandwiches and fruit in the trash. Last spring, our student council weighed the food thrown away in the cafeteria for one week. The total was more than two hundred pounds.

Some teachers worry that students will come to lunch hot and tired after playing. They also say that switching the schedule would take extra planning for cafeteria workers. These are fair concerns. However, schools that have tried recess first report that students simply wash their hands, sit down, and eat calmly because there is nothing left to hurry toward.

Recess first may also help in the classroom. Students who have already run around and then eaten a full meal are more ready to focus during afternoon lessons. Hungry students, on the other hand, often find it hard to pay attention.

Changing the order of two parts of the day costs almost nothing. It could mean less wasted food and calmer afternoons. I hope parents and students will write to the board and ask for a one-year trial.$q$, 'draft')
