-- Passage rg6-authors-purpose-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('3ca7b49f-54a3-590a-9801-d96b1798b506', 'rg6-authors-purpose-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Why Our League Stopped Keeping Score$q$, $q$Three years ago, the Riverside youth soccer league made a change for its youngest players. In games for children under eight, referees stopped keeping score. There are no standings and no trophies for first place. As the league director, I helped make this decision, and I have watched what happened since.

Some parents were upset at first. They argued that children need to learn how to win and lose, and that keeping score makes games exciting. I understand that view, and I agree that older players should play for a score. Learning to handle a loss is a real skill.

For children who are five or six, though, I have seen a different picture. When we kept score, coaches tended to put their fastest players on the field for most of the game. Slower or newer players sat on the bench. Now coaches rotate everyone, and every child gets the same amount of playing time. Our coaches report that more children are trying new moves, like dribbling with their weaker foot, because one mistake no longer feels like a disaster.

The numbers support this, too. Before the change, about one in four of our youngest players did not sign up again the next season. Now fewer than one in ten leave.

Children still know who scored a goal. They cheer and celebrate. The difference is that the game belongs to all of them, not only the stars. When these players turn nine, they will join scored games with stronger skills and a love of the sport.$q$, 'draft')
