-- Passage rg6-authors-purpose-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('86ddb0ce-77a6-5278-ae87-3764d4d439a1', 'rg6-authors-purpose-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$In Praise of the Messy Sketchbook$q$, $q$Many students I know will not let anyone see their sketchbooks. They tear out pages with crooked lines and erase every mistake until the paper wears thin. I understand the feeling, but I want to make a case for the messy sketchbook.

A sketchbook is not a gallery. It is closer to a workshop, a place where ideas are tested before they are finished. Professional artists often fill pages with quick drawings of hands, faces, and objects. Many of these drawings are rough, and some are simply wrong. That is the point. Each attempt teaches the artist something about shape, light, or balance.

When you erase every mistake, you erase the record of your own learning. A page with three clumsy drawings of a cat, followed by a fourth that finally looks right, shows exactly how you improved. Months later, you can flip back and see how far you have come. A sketchbook full of only perfect drawings tells you very little.

Messy pages can also lead to new ideas. A smudge might suggest a shadow you had not planned. A line drawn in the wrong place might turn into the edge of a hill or the curve of a wave.

So keep your crooked lines. Date your pages, fill them quickly, and resist the urge to tear anything out. Your sketchbook should be a place where you are allowed to be a beginner.$q$, 'draft')
