-- Passage rg6-authors-purpose-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('37f66543-ece6-5141-a4d1-450198018115', 'rg6-authors-purpose-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$A Better Way to Visit the Ducks$q$, $q$Feeding bread to the ducks at Willow Pond has been a family tradition for many visitors. Children toss crusts from the dock, and the ducks paddle over in a noisy crowd. It looks like a kind thing to do. As a naturalist at the park, I see the problem that the cheerful scene hides.

Bread fills a duck's stomach, but it gives the bird very little of what it needs to stay healthy. A duck that eats mostly bread may skip the plants, seeds, and insects that keep it strong. Young ducks that eat too much bread can even develop wing problems that make it hard for them to fly.

Leftover bread causes trouble in the water as well. Pieces that the ducks do not eat sink and rot. As the bread breaks down, it feeds tiny green algae that can cover the pond and make the water cloudy and smelly. Large crowds of ducks gathered in one spot also leave more waste behind, which harms the water further.

I do not want families to stop visiting the ducks. Watching them is one of the best parts of a day at Willow Pond. Instead, visitors can bring small amounts of better food, such as thawed frozen peas, chopped lettuce, or birdseed. Even better, they can simply watch the ducks dive and search for food on their own.

The ducks will be just as fun to see, and the pond will stay healthier for everyone who loves it.$q$, 'draft')
